WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:03.480
- Thanks to strong,
but not too strong data

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:06.240
on inflation, retail
sales, and economic growth

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:08.340
in recent months, the US
has been enjoying what

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:10.800
I like to call "Soft Landing Summer."

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:13.740
But some analysts are saying
that the recession that most

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:15.540
of Wall Street predicted
would hit this year

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:17.010
hasn't been avoided.

00:00:17.010 --> 00:00:18.270
It's just been delayed.

00:00:18.270 --> 00:00:20.310
And there are three big reasons why,

00:00:20.310 --> 00:00:21.789
first, some major

00:00:21.789 --> 00:00:24.900
and unexpected government
stimulus programs

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:27.870
that have held up the
economy are going away.

00:00:27.870 --> 00:00:29.790
Second, those big piles

00:00:29.790 --> 00:00:32.520
of pandemic savings have
been largely exhausted

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:34.320
for lower income households.

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:37.740
And third, the data we've
been seeing this summer

00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:40.050
could mean the Fed has more work to do.

00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:41.310
Let's get into it.

00:00:41.310 --> 00:00:43.710
Coming into 2023, it looked like there

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:46.530
wouldn't be any new stimulus
coming out of Washington

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:48.480
because Republicans and Democrats

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:50.160
had split control of Congress.

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:52.290
That usually means nothing gets done

00:00:52.290 --> 00:00:54.270
but because inflation was so high,

00:00:54.270 --> 00:00:56.250
government programs automatically made

00:00:56.250 --> 00:00:59.160
so-called cost of living
adjustments, which meant

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:01.620
that a lot more money was
going out to Americans

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:03.210
receiving government transfers.

00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:05.610
The record setting 8.7% increase

00:01:05.610 --> 00:01:08.310
in social security benefits
made headlines, but

00:01:08.310 --> 00:01:10.890
that wasn't the only program
to get a major increase

00:01:10.890 --> 00:01:11.723
in funds.

00:01:11.723 --> 00:01:14.580
SNAP benefits for food
programs, retirement payments

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:16.672
from federal and state
programs, and the pay

00:01:16.672 --> 00:01:19.890
for government contractors
also went up substantially

00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:21.720
because these automatic increases.

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:23.820
That has meant a lot more money flowing

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:26.160
through the economy in 2023.

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:28.980
And because inflation has
slowed down so much this year,

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:32.580
those cost increases
won't be repeated in 2024.

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:34.650
Ed Clissold, the Chief US strategist

00:01:34.650 --> 00:01:36.990
at Ned Davis Research also points out

00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:38.520
that the effects of the CHIPS Act

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:41.520
and infrastructure bills that
pumped hundreds of billions

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:44.340
of dollars into the
economy are now being felt.

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:46.200
2024 is an election year

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:48.810
and with inflation still
a major talking point,

00:01:48.810 --> 00:01:50.340
Ed doesn't expect to see a repeat

00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:52.140
of those programs next year either.

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:54.610
Many of the lower income
people who rely on

00:01:55.547 --> 00:01:57.090
government programs
have also exhausted much

00:01:57.090 --> 00:01:59.430
or all of the pandemic
savings they built up

00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:01.920
over the past few years,
the amount of savings folks

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:02.753
had put away

00:02:02.753 --> 00:02:05.520
from government stimulus
checks, enhanced unemployment

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:09.690
and just not being able to go
out and spend is much lower.

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:11.880
Brian Rose, senior US Economist

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:13.950
at UBS Global Wealth Management

00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:17.850
says the US personal savings
rate is unsustainably low.

00:02:17.850 --> 00:02:19.650
The saving rate has dropped not just

00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:22.290
below the record levels
we saw during the pandemic

00:02:22.290 --> 00:02:25.230
but even below its average
over the past 10 years.

00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:27.690
Moody's Chief Economist,
Mark Zandi tells me

00:02:27.690 --> 00:02:28.980
that new data show

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:31.020
Americans at lower income tiers

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:32.340
are having more trouble paying

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:35.100
for household expenses than
at the beginning of the year.

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:38.040
And more Americans across
the board are having to find

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:41.070
irregular income sources to
meet their spending needs.

00:02:41.070 --> 00:02:44.100
The US economy is built
on consumers buying

00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:46.560
and that's likely to
slow down if Americans

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:47.940
have spent down their savings.

00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:49.860
We're also seeing big increases

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:51.482
in American's credit card balances

00:02:51.482 --> 00:02:54.150
and in credit card delinquencies.

00:02:54.150 --> 00:02:57.726
Those two things happening
together is not great.

00:02:57.726 --> 00:03:01.860
Last but not least, there's
the Fed, chair Jerome Powell

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:03.900
and the Federal Reserve have already

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:05.880
raised US interest rates to the highest

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:07.320
in more than 20 years.

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:09.780
But with the Fed determined
to get inflation back down

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:10.978
to its 2% target

00:03:10.978 --> 00:03:15.144
and recent US GDP numbers
showing unexpected growth,

00:03:15.144 --> 00:03:17.880
the Fed may have to keep
raising interest rates,

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:19.650
that would put even more pressure

00:03:19.650 --> 00:03:22.223
on US companies,
especially small businesses

00:03:22.223 --> 00:03:24.480
which are the economy's driving force.

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:26.370
Small business optimism has been below

00:03:26.370 --> 00:03:29.759
its nearly 50-year average
for 19 straight months

00:03:29.759 --> 00:03:32.400
making things even tougher
for small companies,

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:35.070
the Fed's latest survey
shows banks have tightened

00:03:35.070 --> 00:03:36.570
their standards for making loans

00:03:36.570 --> 00:03:38.970
to levels that have consistently
signaled a recession

00:03:38.970 --> 00:03:40.920
for the past 50 years.

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:42.600
So here's the big picture,

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:46.260
in 2023, inflation has slowed
down and the US economy

00:03:46.260 --> 00:03:47.520
has held strong.

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:50.280
But both of those things
could be unraveling,

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:52.560
if inflation holds at its current levels

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:55.263
and consumer and business
spending keep falling,

00:03:55.263 --> 00:03:56.910
that recession everyone predicted

00:03:56.910 --> 00:04:00.741
in 2023 could just materialize in 2024.

00:04:00.741 --> 00:04:03.241
(soft music)

